<id/13/1346/#concept> a <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E55_Type>, skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:label "Cubebs"@en ;
    skos:broader <id/grp/grp_drugs_medicinal_botanicals/#concept> ;
    skos:definition "(Ger. kubeben; Fr. cubèbes; Ital. cubebi; Span. cubebas; Russ. kubebû; Arab. kebabeh; Javan. kumunkus; Hin. cubab-chinie). The produce of a plant, the *Piper cubeba* of Linnæus, and, under the Natural System, a species of *Piperaceæ*. It is cultivated in Java, and grows also in Penang and other parts of the East Indies. Specimens of cubebs from Ceylon were shown in the Exhibition of 1862. It is a small unripe fruit, dried like a pepper-corn, but somewhat longer. Cubebs have a hot, pungent, aromatic, slightly bitter taste, and a characteristic odour. They should be chosen large, fresh, sound, and the heaviest that can be procured. When cubebs have been kept long, especially in powder, they lose a portion of their volatile oil, on which, it appears, their medicinal properties depend. [J. R. McCulloch, Dictionary of Commerce and Commercial Navigation (1880), scan p. 555]"@en ;
    skos:editorialNote "Curated classification (group=grp_drugs_medicinal_botanicals): Named materia medica (castoreum, ginseng, cubebs, scammony, maidenhair, liquorice juice, quassia, cocculus indicus, serpentary, asafoetida, cantharides, hellebore, pipsissewa, ipecacuanha) left in the residual bin. Layer-1 observations: 13 rows, 1856–1866. Series: HCPP_Annual Statement of Trade ×13. Units: Lbs. (Pounds) ×13. Top routes: World → United Kingdom ×3; Other Parts → United Kingdom ×3; Holland → United Kingdom ×2; France → United Kingdom ×1."@en ;
    skos:inScheme <id/scheme/#resource> ;
    skos:notation "1346" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Cubebs"@en ;
